{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"api","children":[{"type":"text","value":"API"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../api/core/table.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table API"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-definitions-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Definitions Guide"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column defs are the single most important part of building a table. They are responsible for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Building the underlying data model that will be used for everything including sorting, filtering, grouping, etc."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Formatting the data model into what will be displayed in the table"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating "},{"type":"link","href":"../api/core/header-group.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"header groups"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"../api/core/header.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"headers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"../api/core/column-def.md#footer","children":[{"type":"text","value":"footers"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating columns for display-only purposes, eg. action buttons, checkboxes, expanders, sparklines, etc."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-def-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Def Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The following \"types\" of column defs aren't actually TypeScript types, but more so a way to talk about and describe overall categories of column defs:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Accessor Columns"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessor columns have an underlying data model which means they can be sorted, filtered, grouped, etc."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Display Columns"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Display columns do "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" have a data model which means they cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, but they can be used to display arbitrary content in the table, eg. a row actions button, checkbox, expander, etc."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Grouping Columns"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group columns do "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" have a data model so they too cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, and are used to group other columns together. It's common to define a header or footer for a column group."}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-helpers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Helpers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While column defs are just plain objects at the end of the day, a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createColumnHelper"},{"type":"text","value":" function is exposed from the table core which, when called with a row type, returns a utility for creating different column definition types with the highest type-safety possible."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's an example of creating and using a column helper:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Define your row shape\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<Person>()\n\n// Make some columns!\nconst defaultColumns = [\n  // Display Column\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    cell: props => <RowActions row={props.row} />,\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Name',\n    footer: props => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n        cell: info => info.getValue(),\n        footer: props => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor(row => row.lastName, {\n        id: 'lastName',\n        cell: info => info.getValue(),\n        header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n        footer: props => props.column.id,\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Info',\n    footer: props => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('age', {\n        header: () => 'Age',\n        footer: props => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Grouping Column\n      columnHelper.group({\n        header: 'More Info',\n        columns: [\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('visits', {\n            header: () => <span>Visits</span>,\n            footer: props => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('status', {\n            header: 'Status',\n            footer: props => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('progress', {\n            header: 'Profile Progress',\n            footer: props => props.column.id,\n          }),\n        ],\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n]","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"creating-accessor-columns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating Accessor Columns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data columns are unique in that they must be configured to extract primitive values for each item in your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" array."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are 3 ways to do this:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your items are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"objects"},{"type":"text","value":", use an object-key that corresponds to the value you want to extract."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your items are nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"arrays"},{"type":"text","value":", use an array index that corresponds to the value you want to extract."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use an accessor function that returns the value you want to extract."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"object-keys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Object Keys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If each of your items is an object with the following shape:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You could extract the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"firstName"},{"type":"text","value":" value like so:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName')\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 'firstName',\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"array-indices","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Array Indices"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If each of your items is an array with the following shape:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type Sales = [Date, number]","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You could extract the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"},{"type":"text","value":" value like so:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"columnHelper.accessor(1)\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 1,\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"accessor-functions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessor Functions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If each of your items is an object with the following shape:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You could extract a computed full-name value like so:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"columnHelper.accessor(row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`, {\n  id: 'fullName',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  id: 'fullName',\n  accessorFn: row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`,\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🧠 Remember, the accessed value is what is used to sort, filter, etc. so you'll want to make sure your accessor function returns a primitive value that can be manipulated in a meaningful way. If you return a non-primitive value like an object or array, you will need the appropriate filter/sort/grouping functions to manipulate them, or even supply your own! 😬"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"unique-column-ids","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unique Column IDs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Columns are uniquely identified with 3 strategies:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If defining an accessor column with an object key or array index, the same will be used to uniquely identify the column."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Any periods ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"."},{"type":"text","value":") in an object key will be replaced by underscores ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"_"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If defining an accessor column with an accessor function"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The columns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" property will be used to uniquely identify the column OR"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a primitive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"},{"type":"text","value":" header is supplied, that header string will be used to uniquely identify the column"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🧠 An easy way to remember: If you define a column with an accessor function, either provide a string header or provide a unique "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" property."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-formatting-rendering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Formatting & Rendering"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, columns cells will display their data model value as a string. You can override this behavior by providing custom rendering implementations. Each implementation is provided relevant information about the cell, header or footer and returns something your framework adapter can render eg. JSX/Components/strings/etc. This will depend on which adapter you are using."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are a couple of formatters available to you:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":": Used for formatting cells."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregatedCell"},{"type":"text","value":": Used for formatting cells when aggregated."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":": Used for formatting headers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"footer"},{"type":"text","value":": Used for formatting footers."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"cell-formatting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell Formatting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can provide a custom cell formatter by passing a function to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":" property and using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"props.getValue()"},{"type":"text","value":" function to access your cell's value:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: props => <span>{props.getValue().toUpperCase()}</span>,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell formatters are also provided the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table"},{"type":"text","value":" objects, allowing you to customize the cell formatting beyond just the cell value. The example below provides "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"firstName"},{"type":"text","value":" as the accessor, but also displays a prefixed user ID located on the original row object:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: props => (\n    <span>{`${props.row.original.id} - ${props.getValue()}`}</span>\n  ),\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"aggregated-cell-formatting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aggregated Cell Formatting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For more info on aggregated cells, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./grouping.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"grouping"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"header-footer-formatting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Header & Footer Formatting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Headers and footers do not have access to row data, but still use the same concepts for displaying custom content."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"api","text":"API","level":2},{"id":"column-definitions-guide","text":"Column Definitions Guide","level":2},{"id":"column-def-types","text":"Column Def Types","level":2},{"id":"column-helpers","text":"Column Helpers","level":2},{"id":"creating-accessor-columns","text":"Creating Accessor Columns","level":2},{"id":"object-keys","text":"Object Keys","level":2},{"id":"array-indices","text":"Array Indices","level":2},{"id":"accessor-functions","text":"Accessor Functions","level":2},{"id":"unique-column-ids","text":"Unique Column IDs","level":2},{"id":"column-formatting-rendering","text":"Column Formatting & Rendering","level":2},{"id":"cell-formatting","text":"Cell Formatting","level":2},{"id":"aggregated-cell-formatting","text":"Aggregated Cell Formatting","level":2},{"id":"header-footer-formatting","text":"Header & Footer Formatting","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## API\n\n[Table API](../api/core/table.md)\n\n## Column Definitions Guide\n\nColumn defs are the single most important part of building a table. They are responsible for:\n\n- Building the underlying data model that will be used for everything including sorting, filtering, grouping, etc.\n- Formatting the data model into what will be displayed in the table\n- Creating [header groups](../api/core/header-group.md), [headers](../api/core/header.md) and [footers](../api/core/column-def.md#footer)\n- Creating columns for display-only purposes, eg. action buttons, checkboxes, expanders, sparklines, etc.\n\n## Column Def Types\n\nThe following \"types\" of column defs aren't actually TypeScript types, but more so a way to talk about and describe overall categories of column defs:\n\n- `Accessor Columns`\n  - Accessor columns have an underlying data model which means they can be sorted, filtered, grouped, etc.\n- `Display Columns`\n  - Display columns do **not** have a data model which means they cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, but they can be used to display arbitrary content in the table, eg. a row actions button, checkbox, expander, etc.\n- `Grouping Columns`\n  - Group columns do **not** have a data model so they too cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, and are used to group other columns together. It's common to define a header or footer for a column group.\n\n## Column Helpers\n\nWhile column defs are just plain objects at the end of the day, a `createColumnHelper` function is exposed from the table core which, when called with a row type, returns a utility for creating different column definition types with the highest type-safety possible.\n\nHere's an example of creating and using a column helper:\n\n```tsx\n// Define your row shape\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<Person>()\n\n// Make some columns!\nconst defaultColumns = [\n  // Display Column\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    cell: props => <RowActions row={props.row} />,\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Name',\n    footer: props => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n        cell: info => info.getValue(),\n        footer: props => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor(row => row.lastName, {\n        id: 'lastName',\n        cell: info => info.getValue(),\n        header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n        footer: props => props.column.id,\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Info',\n    footer: props => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('age', {\n        header: () => 'Age',\n        footer: props => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Grouping Column\n      columnHelper.group({\n        header: 'More Info',\n        columns: [\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('visits', {\n            header: () => <span>Visits</span>,\n            footer: props => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('status', {\n            header: 'Status',\n            footer: props => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('progress', {\n            header: 'Profile Progress',\n            footer: props => props.column.id,\n          }),\n        ],\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n]\n```\n\n## Creating Accessor Columns\n\nData columns are unique in that they must be configured to extract primitive values for each item in your `data` array.\n\nThere are 3 ways to do this:\n\n- If your items are `objects`, use an object-key that corresponds to the value you want to extract.\n- If your items are nested `arrays`, use an array index that corresponds to the value you want to extract.\n- Use an accessor function that returns the value you want to extract.\n\n## Object Keys\n\nIf each of your items is an object with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n```\n\nYou could extract the `firstName` value like so:\n\n```tsx\n\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName')\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 'firstName',\n}\n```\n\n## Array Indices\n\nIf each of your items is an array with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Sales = [Date, number]\n```\n\nYou could extract the `number` value like so:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor(1)\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 1,\n}\n```\n\n## Accessor Functions\n\nIf each of your items is an object with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n```\n\nYou could extract a computed full-name value like so:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor(row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`, {\n  id: 'fullName',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  id: 'fullName',\n  accessorFn: row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`,\n}\n```\n\n> 🧠 Remember, the accessed value is what is used to sort, filter, etc. so you'll want to make sure your accessor function returns a primitive value that can be manipulated in a meaningful way. If you return a non-primitive value like an object or array, you will need the appropriate filter/sort/grouping functions to manipulate them, or even supply your own! 😬\n\n## Unique Column IDs\n\nColumns are uniquely identified with 3 strategies:\n\n- If defining an accessor column with an object key or array index, the same will be used to uniquely identify the column.\n  - Any periods (`.`) in an object key will be replaced by underscores (`_`).\n- If defining an accessor column with an accessor function\n  - The columns `id` property will be used to uniquely identify the column OR\n  - If a primitive `string` header is supplied, that header string will be used to uniquely identify the column\n\n> 🧠 An easy way to remember: If you define a column with an accessor function, either provide a string header or provide a unique `id` property.\n\n## Column Formatting & Rendering\n\nBy default, columns cells will display their data model value as a string. You can override this behavior by providing custom rendering implementations. Each implementation is provided relevant information about the cell, header or footer and returns something your framework adapter can render eg. JSX/Components/strings/etc. This will depend on which adapter you are using.\n\nThere are a couple of formatters available to you:\n\n- `cell`: Used for formatting cells.\n- `aggregatedCell`: Used for formatting cells when aggregated.\n- `header`: Used for formatting headers.\n- `footer`: Used for formatting footers.\n\n## Cell Formatting\n\nYou can provide a custom cell formatter by passing a function to the `cell` property and using the `props.getValue()` function to access your cell's value:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: props => <span>{props.getValue().toUpperCase()}</span>,\n})\n```\n\nCell formatters are also provided the `row` and `table` objects, allowing you to customize the cell formatting beyond just the cell value. The example below provides `firstName` as the accessor, but also displays a prefixed user ID located on the original row object:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: props => (\n    <span>{`${props.row.original.id} - ${props.getValue()}`}</span>\n  ),\n})\n```\n\n## Aggregated Cell Formatting\n\nFor more info on aggregated cells, see [grouping](./grouping.md).\n\n## Header & Footer Formatting\n\nHeaders and footers do not have access to row data, but still use the same concepts for displaying custom content.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Columns Guide\n---\n\n## API\n\n[Table API](../api/core/table.md)\n\n## Column Definitions Guide\n\nColumn defs are the single most important part of building a table. They are responsible for:\n\n- Building the underlying data model that will be used for everything including sorting, filtering, grouping, etc.\n- Formatting the data model into what will be displayed in the table\n- Creating [header groups](../api/core/header-group.md), [headers](../api/core/header.md) and [footers](../api/core/column-def.md#footer)\n- Creating columns for display-only purposes, eg. action buttons, checkboxes, expanders, sparklines, etc.\n\n## Column Def Types\n\nThe following \"types\" of column defs aren't actually TypeScript types, but more so a way to talk about and describe overall categories of column defs:\n\n- `Accessor Columns`\n  - Accessor columns have an underlying data model which means they can be sorted, filtered, grouped, etc.\n- `Display Columns`\n  - Display columns do **not** have a data model which means they cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, but they can be used to display arbitrary content in the table, eg. a row actions button, checkbox, expander, etc.\n- `Grouping Columns`\n  - Group columns do **not** have a data model so they too cannot be sorted, filtered, etc, and are used to group other columns together. It's common to define a header or footer for a column group.\n\n## Column Helpers\n\nWhile column defs are just plain objects at the end of the day, a `createColumnHelper` function is exposed from the table core which, when called with a row type, returns a utility for creating different column definition types with the highest type-safety possible.\n\nHere's an example of creating and using a column helper:\n\n```tsx\n// Define your row shape\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<Person>()\n\n// Make some columns!\nconst defaultColumns = [\n  // Display Column\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    cell: props => <RowActions row={props.row} />,\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Name',\n    footer: props => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n        cell: info => info.getValue(),\n        footer: props => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor(row => row.lastName, {\n        id: 'lastName',\n        cell: info => info.getValue(),\n        header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n        footer: props => props.column.id,\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n  // Grouping Column\n  columnHelper.group({\n    header: 'Info',\n    footer: props => props.column.id,\n    columns: [\n      // Accessor Column\n      columnHelper.accessor('age', {\n        header: () => 'Age',\n        footer: props => props.column.id,\n      }),\n      // Grouping Column\n      columnHelper.group({\n        header: 'More Info',\n        columns: [\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('visits', {\n            header: () => <span>Visits</span>,\n            footer: props => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('status', {\n            header: 'Status',\n            footer: props => props.column.id,\n          }),\n          // Accessor Column\n          columnHelper.accessor('progress', {\n            header: 'Profile Progress',\n            footer: props => props.column.id,\n          }),\n        ],\n      }),\n    ],\n  }),\n]\n```\n\n## Creating Accessor Columns\n\nData columns are unique in that they must be configured to extract primitive values for each item in your `data` array.\n\nThere are 3 ways to do this:\n\n- If your items are `objects`, use an object-key that corresponds to the value you want to extract.\n- If your items are nested `arrays`, use an array index that corresponds to the value you want to extract.\n- Use an accessor function that returns the value you want to extract.\n\n## Object Keys\n\nIf each of your items is an object with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n```\n\nYou could extract the `firstName` value like so:\n\n```tsx\n\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName')\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 'firstName',\n}\n```\n\n## Array Indices\n\nIf each of your items is an array with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Sales = [Date, number]\n```\n\nYou could extract the `number` value like so:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor(1)\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  accessorKey: 1,\n}\n```\n\n## Accessor Functions\n\nIf each of your items is an object with the following shape:\n\n```tsx\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  status: string\n  progress: number\n}\n```\n\nYou could extract a computed full-name value like so:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor(row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`, {\n  id: 'fullName',\n})\n\n// OR\n\n{\n  id: 'fullName',\n  accessorFn: row => `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`,\n}\n```\n\n> 🧠 Remember, the accessed value is what is used to sort, filter, etc. so you'll want to make sure your accessor function returns a primitive value that can be manipulated in a meaningful way. If you return a non-primitive value like an object or array, you will need the appropriate filter/sort/grouping functions to manipulate them, or even supply your own! 😬\n\n## Unique Column IDs\n\nColumns are uniquely identified with 3 strategies:\n\n- If defining an accessor column with an object key or array index, the same will be used to uniquely identify the column.\n  - Any periods (`.`) in an object key will be replaced by underscores (`_`).\n- If defining an accessor column with an accessor function\n  - The columns `id` property will be used to uniquely identify the column OR\n  - If a primitive `string` header is supplied, that header string will be used to uniquely identify the column\n\n> 🧠 An easy way to remember: If you define a column with an accessor function, either provide a string header or provide a unique `id` property.\n\n## Column Formatting & Rendering\n\nBy default, columns cells will display their data model value as a string. You can override this behavior by providing custom rendering implementations. Each implementation is provided relevant information about the cell, header or footer and returns something your framework adapter can render eg. JSX/Components/strings/etc. This will depend on which adapter you are using.\n\nThere are a couple of formatters available to you:\n\n- `cell`: Used for formatting cells.\n- `aggregatedCell`: Used for formatting cells when aggregated.\n- `header`: Used for formatting headers.\n- `footer`: Used for formatting footers.\n\n## Cell Formatting\n\nYou can provide a custom cell formatter by passing a function to the `cell` property and using the `props.getValue()` function to access your cell's value:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: props => <span>{props.getValue().toUpperCase()}</span>,\n})\n```\n\nCell formatters are also provided the `row` and `table` objects, allowing you to customize the cell formatting beyond just the cell value. The example below provides `firstName` as the accessor, but also displays a prefixed user ID located on the original row object:\n\n```tsx\ncolumnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n  cell: props => (\n    <span>{`${props.row.original.id} - ${props.getValue()}`}</span>\n  ),\n})\n```\n\n## Aggregated Cell Formatting\n\nFor more info on aggregated cells, see [grouping](./grouping.md).\n\n## Header & Footer Formatting\n\nHeaders and footers do not have access to row data, but still use the same concepts for displaying custom content.\n"}
